Let $f(x)=x^4-4x^3+6x^2-4x-2005.$ The rational root theorem says the possible rational roots i of f are $\pm1,\pm5,\pm401,\pm2005.$ If f is divisible by x-r for some rational r, then r is an integer. By the rational root theorem, f is divisible by $(x-1)$ and $(x-5).$ If f is divisible by $(x-1).(x-5)$ then
\begin{align*}
f(x) & =(x^2-6x+5)g(x) \\
&=(x-5)(x-1)g(x)\end{align*}
Since f(0)=-2005, since g must be a fourth degree polynomial with rational coefficients, we have that g(0)=-2005/10=-200.5. Thus, we have a rational root. $\textbf{contradiction}.$ Therefore, f has only 2 roots. The sum of the roots is 4. So, the product of the nonreal roots is 2005.</think> <answer>2005</answer>

